<section id="ch1" class="chapter">
  <div class="ch-num">Chapter 1</div>
  <h2>Statistical Decision Theory, the Exact Bias–Variance Decomposition, and Learning Theory</h2>

  <p>Machine learning begins as a problem of statistical decision theory (Wald, 1950): an input $x \in \mathcal{X}$ and an output $y \in \mathcal{Y}$ are drawn jointly from an unknown probability measure $P$ on $\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Y}$. A learner observes a finite sample $S = \{(x_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^n \sim P^n$ (plus any internal algorithmic randomness $\xi$) and produces a decision rule $\hat{f}_S : \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{A}$ inside a hypothesis class $\mathcal{H}$. Given a measurable loss function $\ell : \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{Y} \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$, the <strong>population risk</strong> and <strong>empirical risk</strong> are</p>

  <div class="eq-box">$$R(f) \;=\; \mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim P}\big[\ell(f(X), Y)\big], \qquad \widehat{R}_S(f) \;=\; \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \ell\big(f(x_i), y_i\big).$$</div>

  <h3 id="sec-1-1">1.1 Bayes Risk, Proper Scoring Rules, and Pointwise Optimal Predictors</h3>
  <p>Before selecting any hypothesis class $\mathcal{H}$ or observing any training data $S$, the data-generating distribution $P$ fixes the absolute lower bound on achievable risk: the <strong>Bayes risk</strong> $R^* = \inf_{f \text{ measurable}} R(f)$. Because $R(f) = \mathbb{E}_X\big[\mathbb{E}_{Y\mid X}[\ell(f(X), Y) \mid X]\big]$, any measurable $f^*$ minimizing the conditional risk $\mathbb{E}[\ell(a, Y) \mid X = x]$ pointwise for $P_X$-almost every $x$ achieves $R^*$. Solving this pointwise minimization yields the canonical Bayes predictors:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Squared error $\ell(a, y) = (a - y)^2$:</strong> Setting $\frac{\partial}{\partial a}\mathbb{E}[(a - Y)^2 \mid X=x] = 2(a - \mathbb{E}[Y\mid X=x]) = 0$ yields the regression function $f^*(x) = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid X = x]$, with irreducible pointwise variance $\sigma^2(x) = \mathrm{Var}(Y \mid X = x)$ and Bayes risk $R^* = \mathbb{E}_X[\sigma^2(X)]$.</li>
    <li><strong>Absolute ($\ell_1$) error $\ell(a, y) = |a - y|$:</strong> The subdifferential condition $0 \in \partial_a \mathbb{E}[|a - Y| \mid X=x]$ is equivalent to $\mathbb{P}(Y \lt a \mid X=x) \le \tfrac{1}{2} \le \mathbb{P}(Y \le a \mid X=x)$, so $f^*(x) = \mathrm{median}(Y \mid X = x)$. More generally, the pinball loss $\ell_\tau(a, y) = \max(\tau(y-a), (\tau-1)(y-a))$ recovers the $\tau$-th conditional quantile (Koenker &amp; Bassett, 1978).</li>
    <li><strong>0–1 classification loss $\ell(a, y) = \mathbf{1}[a \ne y]$:</strong> Conditional risk is $1 - \mathbb{P}(Y = a \mid X = x)$, minimized by the Bayes classifier $f^*(x) = \arg\max_{c \in \mathcal{Y}} \mathbb{P}(Y = c \mid X = x)$ with Bayes error $R^* = \mathbb{E}_X[1 - \max_c \mathbb{P}(Y = c \mid X)]$.</li>
    <li><strong>Cost-sensitive classification:</strong> For asymmetric costs $C_{ij}$ of predicting $i$ when truth is $j$, the Bayes action is $\arg\min_i \sum_j C_{ij} \mathbb{P}(Y = j \mid X = x)$; in binary classification with false-positive cost $c_{\mathrm{FP}}$ and false-negative cost $c_{\mathrm{FN}}$, the optimal threshold shifts from $\tfrac{1}{2}$ to $c_{\mathrm{FP}} / (c_{\mathrm{FP}} + c_{\mathrm{FN}})$.</li>
    <li><strong>Log-loss / cross-entropy $\ell(q, y) = -\log q(y)$:</strong> For any probability forecast $q \in \Delta(\mathcal{Y})$, $\mathbb{E}[-\log q(Y) \mid X=x] = H(P(\cdot\mid x)) + D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P(\cdot\mid x)\,\|\,q)$. By Gibbs' inequality ($D_{\mathrm{KL}} \ge 0$ with equality iff $q = P(\cdot\mid x)$), log-loss is a <strong>strictly proper scoring rule</strong> (Gneiting &amp; Raftery, 2007), uniquely recovering true calibrated conditional probabilities with Bayes risk equal to conditional Shannon entropy $H(Y \mid X)$.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For squared error, <em>every</em> fixed predictor $\hat{f}$ — before taking any expectation over datasets $S$ — satisfies an exact Hilbert-space Pythagorean decomposition in $L^2(P)$:</p>
  <div class="eq-box">$$R(\hat{f}) \;=\; \mathbb{E}\big[(\hat{f}(X) - Y)^2\big] \;=\; \underbrace{\mathbb{E}\big[(Y - f^*(X))^2\big]}_{R^* \;=\; \text{Bayes (irreducible) noise}} \;+\; \underbrace{\mathbb{E}\big[(\hat{f}(X) - f^*(X))^2\big]}_{\text{excess risk } \mathcal{E}(\hat{f})},$$</div>
  <p>because $Y - f^*(X)$ is orthogonal to every $\sigma(X)$-measurable square-integrable function: $\mathbb{E}[(\hat{f}(X) - f^*(X))(f^*(X) - Y)] = \mathbb{E}_X[(\hat{f}(X) - f^*(X))\,\mathbb{E}[f^*(X) - Y \mid X]] = 0$.</p>

  <h3 id="sec-1-2">1.2 The Exact Bias–Variance–Noise Decomposition — and Five Things Courses Consistently Misteach</h3>
  <p>Now treat the learner $\mathcal{A}$ as a map from a random training set $S \sim P^n$ and algorithmic randomness $\xi$ (initialization, SGD minibatch shuffling, dropout masks, random projections) to a random predictor $\hat{f}_{S,\xi}$. Fix a test point $x \in \mathcal{X}$ and let $\bar{f}(x) = \mathbb{E}_{S,\xi}[\hat{f}_{S,\xi}(x)]$ denote the expected predictor across draws of $(S, \xi)$. Adding and subtracting $\bar{f}(x)$ inside the excess risk yields the exact squared-loss identity:</p>

  <div class="thm-box">
    <div class="thm-head"><span>Theorem 1.1 · Exact Squared-Loss Bias–Variance–Noise Decomposition</span><span>Geman, Bienenstock &amp; Doursat (1992)</span></div>
    <p>For any joint distribution $P$ with $\mathbb{E}[Y^2] \lt \infty$, any test point $x \in \mathcal{X}$, and any learning procedure whose output $\hat{f}_{S,\xi}(x)$ is independent of the test label $Y \mid X = x$:</p>
    <div class="eq-box">$$\mathbb{E}_{S,\xi,Y}\!\Big[\big(\hat{f}_{S,\xi}(x) - Y\big)^2 \,\Big|\, X=x\Big] \;=\; \underbrace{\sigma^2(x)}_{\text{Irreducible Noise}} \;+\; \underbrace{\big(\bar{f}(x) - f^*(x)\big)^2}_{\mathrm{Bias}^2(\mathcal{A};\, x)} \;+\; \underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{S,\xi}\!\Big[\big(\hat{f}_{S,\xi}(x) - \bar{f}(x)\big)^2\Big]}_{\mathrm{Var}(\mathcal{A};\, x)}.$$</div>
    <p><em>Proof.</em> Conditioning on $(S, \xi)$, the orthogonality $\mathbb{E}[Y - f^*(x) \mid X=x] = 0$ splits the expectation into $\sigma^2(x) + \mathbb{E}_{S,\xi}[(\hat{f}_{S,\xi}(x) - f^*(x))^2]$. Writing $\hat{f}_{S,\xi}(x) - f^*(x) = (\hat{f}_{S,\xi}(x) - \bar{f}(x)) + (\bar{f}(x) - f^*(x))$ and squaring gives $\mathrm{Var} + \mathrm{Bias}^2 + 2(\bar{f}(x) - f^*(x))\,\mathbb{E}_{S,\xi}[\hat{f}_{S,\xi}(x) - \bar{f}(x)]$. The cross-term vanishes identically because $\mathbb{E}_{S,\xi}[\hat{f}_{S,\xi}(x)] = \bar{f}(x)$. Integrating over $X \sim P_X$ preserves all three nonnegative terms. $\square$</p>
  </div>

  <p>Even top undergraduate and graduate machine learning courses (and their multiple-choice exams) routinely misteach at least <strong>five mathematical facts</strong> about this decomposition:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Bias and variance are properties of a <em>learning rule over the sampling distribution</em> $S \sim P^n$, never of a single fitted model — and statistical bias is NOT approximation error.</strong> Given one trained parameter vector $\hat{w}$, its variance across datasets is undefined until you specify the estimator $\mathcal{A}$ and sampling law $P^n$. Moreover, courses constantly confuse the <em>approximation–estimation decomposition</em> of a fixed class $\mathcal{H}$,
    $$R(\hat{f}_S) - R^* \;=\; \underbrace{\big(\inf_{h \in \mathcal{H}} R(h) - R^*\big)}_{\text{approximation error (property of }\mathcal{H}\text{)}} \;+\; \underbrace{\big(R(\hat{f}_S) - \inf_{h \in \mathcal{H}} R(h)\big)}_{\text{estimation error (random in }S\text{)}},$$
    with the <em>bias–variance decomposition</em> of an estimator $\mathcal{A}$. They are completely different decompositions! A model class $\mathcal{H}$ containing $f^*$ has zero approximation error, yet ridge regression, Lasso, early-stopped SGD, or any MAP estimator inside $\mathcal{H}$ has strictly positive statistical bias $\bar{f} \ne f^*$. Conversely, for nonlinear classes, $\bar{f} = \mathbb{E}_S[\hat{f}_S]$ need not even lie inside $\mathcal{H}$, so $\mathrm{Bias}^2$ can actually be <em>smaller</em> than the approximation error of $\mathcal{H}$ (this is the exact reason bagging decision stumps or trees can reduce bias as well as variance!).</li>

    <li><strong>The additive decomposition $\text{Error} = \text{Noise} + \text{Bias}^2 + \text{Variance}$ is FALSE for 0–1 classification loss — where variance can actively <em>reduce</em> test error.</strong> Consider a binary classification point $x$ with deterministic true label $y = +1$ ($\sigma^2 = 0$). Suppose a biased classifier outputs $\hat{y} = -1$ with probability $0.7$ and $+1$ with probability $0.3$ across draws of $S$. Its modal (main) prediction is $y_m = -1 \ne y$, so its zero-one bias is $B(x) = \mathbf{1}[y_m \ne y] = 1$, its zero-one variance is $V(x) = \mathbb{P}(\hat{y} \ne y_m) = 0.3$, and its expected 0–1 error is $\mathbb{P}(\hat{y} \ne y) = 0.7 = B(x) - V(x)$ — <strong>strictly minus variance</strong>! As Domingos (2000) proved in his unified decomposition for 0–1 loss:
    <div class="eq-box">$$\mathbb{E}_{S}\big[\mathbf{1}[\hat{f}_S(x) \ne y]\big] \;=\; B(x) \;+\; \big(1 - 2 B(x)\big)\,V(x) \qquad (\text{binary, deterministic } y).$$</div>
    When the main prediction is right ($B=0$), variance hurts ($+V$). When the main prediction is wrong ($B=1$), variance <em>helps</em> ($-V$) because random fluctuations rescue predictions from the wrong side of the decision boundary. Any exam question claiming "reducing variance always reduces expected test error" or "bagging never hurts a biased classifier" has no correct answer under the textbook premise.</li>

    <li><strong>Bregman divergences are the exact family of losses with an additive bias–variance decomposition — and the centroid is the <em>dual</em> mean, not the arithmetic mean (Heskes, 1998; Pfau, 2013).</strong> For any strictly convex differentiable potential $\phi$, the Bregman divergence $D_\phi(y \| \hat{y}) = \phi(y) - \phi(\hat{y}) - \langle \nabla\phi(\hat{y}), y - \hat{y}\rangle$ (covering squared error, KL / cross-entropy, Itakura–Saito, Mahalanobis) satisfies
    $$\mathbb{E}[D_\phi(Y \| \hat{Y})] \;=\; \mathbb{E}[D_\phi(Y \| f^*)] \;+\; D_\phi(f^* \| \tilde{f}) \;+\; \mathbb{E}[D_\phi(\tilde{f} \| \hat{Y})], \qquad \tilde{f} = (\nabla\phi)^{-1}\!\big(\mathbb{E}[\nabla\phi(\hat{Y})]\big).$$
    For cross-entropy / KL divergence, $\nabla\phi(q)_k = \log q_k + 1$, so the true bias–variance centroid $\tilde{f}_k \propto \exp(\mathbb{E}[\log \hat{q}_k])$ is the normalized <em>geometric</em> mean (logit average), not the arithmetic probability average $\mathbb{E}[\hat{q}]$.</li>

    <li><strong>Fixed-design ridge regression proves James–Stein dominance, NOT a universal monotone complexity trade-off.</strong> In fixed-design linear regression $y = X w^* + \varepsilon$ with $X \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times p}$ ($p \le n$, full column rank) and $\varepsilon \sim (0, \sigma^2 I_n)$, the ridge estimator $\hat{w}_\lambda = (X^\top X + \lambda I)^{-1} X^\top y$ has exact in-sample prediction risk:
    $$\mathrm{Bias}^2(\hat{w}_\lambda) = \frac{\lambda^2}{n}\sum_{j=1}^p \frac{s_j^2}{(s_j^2 + \lambda)^2} (v_j^\top w^*)^2, \qquad \mathrm{Var}(\hat{w}_\lambda) = \frac{\sigma^2}{n}\sum_{j=1}^p \frac{s_j^4}{(s_j^2 + \lambda)^2},$$
    where $X = U \mathrm{diag}(s_1,\dots,s_p) V^\top$. Differentiating at $\lambda = 0$ gives $\frac{d}{d\lambda}\mathrm{Bias}^2\big|_{\lambda=0} = 0$ (second-order penalty $O(\lambda^2)$) whereas $\frac{d}{d\lambda}\mathrm{Var}\big|_{\lambda=0} = -\frac{2\sigma^2}{n}\sum_j s_j^{-2} \lt 0$ (first-order reduction $O(\lambda)$)! Thus for <em>every</em> $w^*$ and $\sigma^2 \gt 0$, there exists $\lambda \gt 0$ with strictly smaller MSE than unbiased OLS (Hoerl &amp; Kennard, 1970; reflecting James &amp; Stein's 1961 proof that the sample mean is inadmissible in $\mathbb{R}^p$ for $p \ge 3$). Note also that in fixed design, OLS is unbiased ($\mathrm{Bias}^2 = 0$), while adding $\lambda$ trades bias against variance monotonically.</li>

    <li><strong>The textbook "U-shaped bias–variance curve" breaks completely in the overparameterized regime $p \gt n$ (Double Descent &amp; Benign Overfitting).</strong> Consider random-design isotropic Gaussian features $x_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I_p)$ and minimum-$\ell_2$-norm least squares $\hat{w}_0 = X^+ y$ (which gradient descent from $0$ converges to automatically). In the proportional limit $p/n \to \gamma$:
    <div class="eq-box">$$\mathrm{Var}(\hat{w}_0) \;=\; \begin{cases} \sigma^2 \dfrac{\gamma}{1 - \gamma}, & \gamma \lt 1 \quad (p \lt n), \\[8pt] \infty, & \gamma = 1 \quad (p = n), \\[8pt] \sigma^2 \dfrac{1}{\gamma - 1}, & \gamma \gt 1 \quad (p \gt n) \end{cases}$$</div>
    (Belkin, Hsu, Ma &amp; Mandal, 2019; Hastie, Montanari, Rosset &amp; Tibshirani, 2022). As $p \to n^-$, the smallest singular value of $X$ collapses toward $0$ (by the Marchenko–Pastur law, $\lambda_{\min}(\frac{1}{n}X^\top X) \to (1 - \sqrt{\gamma})^2 \to 0$), so inverting $X^\top X$ amplifies noise arbitrarily — producing the interpolation spike. Yet once $p \gt n$, the matrix $X X^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ becomes <em>better conditioned</em> as $p$ grows ($\lambda_{\min}(\frac{1}{p} X X^\top) \to (1 - \sqrt{1/\gamma})^2$), so the minimum-norm interpolator spreads label noise across $p - n$ extra orthogonal directions and <strong>variance drops back down toward zero</strong> while bias simultaneously shrinks! When the feature covariance $\Sigma$ has a low-dimensional signal subspace plus a long tail of small eigenvalues (large effective rank $r_k(\Sigma) \gg n$), even zero-training-error interpolation is statistically consistent: <strong>benign overfitting</strong> (Bartlett, Long, Lugosi &amp; Tsigler, 2020).</li>
  </ol>

