  <h3 id="sec-1-3">1.3 Concentration, Uniform Convergence, VC Dimension, and Rademacher Complexity</h3>
  <p>Why does minimizing empirical risk $\widehat{R}_S(h)$ control population risk $R(h)$? For any <em>single</em> fixed hypothesis $h$ with loss bounded in $[0, 1]$, Hoeffding's inequality (1963) gives $\mathbb{P}(|R(h) - \widehat{R}_S(h)| \gt \varepsilon) \le 2 e^{-2n\varepsilon^2}$. Because ERM picks $\hat{h}_S = \arg\min_{h \in \mathcal{H}} \widehat{R}_S(h)$ <em>dependently</em> on $S$, we need a uniform bound on $\sup_{h \in \mathcal{H}} |R(h) - \widehat{R}_S(h)|$. For finite $|\mathcal{H}|$, a union bound immediately gives $|R(\hat{h}_S) - R(h^*)| \le 2\sqrt{\frac{\ln(2|\mathcal{H}|/\delta)}{2n}}$. For infinite classes, Vapnik &amp; Chervonenkis (1971) and Valiant (1984, PAC learning) replaced $\log|\mathcal{H}|$ with the combinatorial <strong>growth function</strong> $\Pi_{\mathcal{H}}(n) = \max_{x_1,\dots,x_n} |\{(h(x_1),\dots,h(x_n)) : h \in \mathcal{H}\}|$ and <strong>VC dimension</strong> $d = \max\{m : \Pi_{\mathcal{H}}(m) = 2^m\}$.</p>

  <div class="thm-box">
    <div class="thm-head"><span>Theorem 1.2 · Sauer–Shelah–Perles Lemma &amp; Fundamental Theorem of PAC Learning</span><span>Vapnik &amp; Chervonenkis (1971); Sauer (1972); Shelah (1972); Blumer et al. (1989)</span></div>
    <p>If $\mathrm{VC}(\mathcal{H}) = d \lt \infty$, then for every $n \ge d$, $\Pi_{\mathcal{H}}(n) \le \sum_{i=0}^d \binom{n}{i} \le \left(\frac{en}{d}\right)^d$ (polynomial in $n$, rather than $2^n$). By ghost-sample symmetrization and McDiarmid's bounded-differences inequality, with probability $\ge 1 - \delta$ over $S \sim P^n$, simultaneously for all $h \in \mathcal{H}$:</p>
    <div class="eq-box">$$R(h) \;\le\; \widehat{R}_S(h) \;+\; C\sqrt{\frac{d\log(en/d) + \log(1/\delta)}{n}}.$$</div>
    <p>Conversely, finite VC dimension is both necessary and sufficient for distribution-free PAC learnability, with minimax sample complexity $\Theta\!\big((d + \log(1/\delta))/\varepsilon^2\big)$ in the agnostic setting and $\Theta\!\big((d + \log(1/\delta))/\varepsilon\big)$ in the realizable setting (Hanneke, 2016).</p>
  </div>

  <p>Concrete VC dimensions illustrate the geometry of hypothesis capacity: linear halfspaces in $\mathbb{R}^p$ have $\mathrm{VC} = p + 1$ (by Radon's theorem); axis-aligned rectangles in $\mathbb{R}^p$ have $\mathrm{VC} = 2p$; piecewise-linear ReLU networks with $W$ weights and $L$ layers have $\mathrm{VC} = \widetilde{\Theta}(WL)$ (Bartlett et al., 2019); whereas the sine family $\{x \mapsto \mathbf{1}[\sin(\omega x) \ge 0] : \omega \in \mathbb{R}\}$ has <em>infinite</em> VC dimension with a single scalar parameter — disproving the naive slogan that capacity equals parameter count.</p>

  <p>For real-valued loss classes $\mathcal{G} = \{(x,y) \mapsto \ell(h(x), y) : h \in \mathcal{H}\} \subseteq [0, 1]^{\mathcal{Z}}$, <strong>empirical Rademacher complexity</strong> (Bartlett &amp; Mendelson, 2002) measures how well $\mathcal{G}$ can align with pure noise $\sigma_i \stackrel{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} \mathrm{Unif}\{\pm 1\}$:</p>
  <div class="eq-box">$$\widehat{\mathfrak{R}}_S(\mathcal{G}) \;=\; \mathbb{E}_{\sigma}\!\left[\sup_{g \in \mathcal{G}} \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \sigma_i g(z_i)\right], \qquad R(h) \;\le\; \widehat{R}_S(h) + 2\widehat{\mathfrak{R}}_S(\mathcal{G}) + 3\sqrt{\frac{\ln(2/\delta)}{2n}}.$$</div>
  <p>By <strong>Talagrand's contraction lemma</strong> (Ledoux &amp; Talagrand, 1991), composing with an $L$-Lipschitz loss scales Rademacher complexity by at most $L$. For norm-bounded linear predictors $\mathcal{F}_B = \{x \mapsto \langle w, x\rangle : \|w\|_2 \le B\}$ on inputs with $\|x_i\|_2 \le R$, Cauchy–Schwarz yields the <em>dimension-free</em> bound</p>
  <div class="eq-box">$$\widehat{\mathfrak{R}}_S(\mathcal{F}_B) \;=\; \frac{1}{n}\mathbb{E}_\sigma\!\left[\sup_{\|w\|_2 \le B} \Big\langle w,\, \sum_{i=1}^n \sigma_i x_i\Big\rangle\right] \;\le\; \frac{B}{n}\sqrt{\mathbb{E}_\sigma\!\left\|\sum_{i=1}^n \sigma_i x_i\right\|_2^2} \;\le\; \frac{BR}{\sqrt{n}}.$$</div>
  <p>The ambient dimension $p$ vanishes completely — which is why infinite-dimensional kernel machines and wide neural networks with large margin can generalize despite having $p \gg n$.</p>

  <h3 id="sec-1-4">1.4 RKHS, Support Vector Machines, Ensembles, and No Free Lunch</h3>
  <p>By <strong>Mercer's theorem</strong> (1909) and the <strong>Moore–Aronszajn theorem</strong> (1950), every symmetric positive semidefinite kernel $k : \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}$ uniquely determines a Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space (RKHS) $\mathcal{H}_k$ with reproducing property $\langle f, k(x,\cdot)\rangle_{\mathcal{H}_k} = f(x)$. By the <strong>Representer Theorem</strong> (Kimeldorf &amp; Wahba, 1971; Schölkopf et al., 2001), any minimizer of $\sum_{i=1}^n \ell(f(x_i), y_i) + \Omega(\|f\|_{\mathcal{H}_k})$ lies in $\mathrm{span}\{k(x_1,\cdot),\dots,k(x_n,\cdot)\}$, reducing infinite-dimensional functional optimization to the $n \times n$ Gram matrix $K_{ij} = k(x_i, x_j)$.</p>
  <p><strong>Support Vector Machines</strong> (Boser, Guyon &amp; Vapnik, 1992; Cortes &amp; Vapnik, 1995) maximize the geometric margin $1/\|w\|_{\mathcal{H}_k}$ under hinge loss $\max(0, 1 - y_i f(x_i))$; by Lagrangian duality and KKT complementary slackness, $\alpha_i \gt 0$ only for <em>support vectors</em> on or inside the margin ($y_i f(x_i) \le 1$), yielding sparse dual solutions with margin-based Rademacher bounds $O(R / (\gamma\sqrt{n}))$. Meanwhile, <strong>ensemble methods</strong> attack the risk decomposition from opposite directions: <strong>Bagging and Random Forests</strong> (Breiman, 1996, 2001) average decorrelated high-variance trees across bootstrap samples to drive variance down by $\rho \sigma^2 + \frac{1-\rho}{B}\sigma^2$, whereas <strong>Boosting</strong> — AdaBoost (Freund &amp; Schapire, 1997) and Gradient Boosting (Friedman, 2001) — performs functional gradient descent in hypothesis space (minimizing exponential loss $\sum_i e^{-y_i F(x_i)}$ or general differentiable losses), simultaneously reducing bias and driving up the normalized margin distribution (Schapire, Freund, Bartlett &amp; Lee, 1998).</p>
  <p>Finally, the <strong>No Free Lunch Theorem</strong> (Wolpert, 1996; Wolpert &amp; Macready, 1997) proves that averaged uniformly over all $2^{|\mathcal{X}|}$ target functions on a finite domain, every learning algorithm has identical off-training-set error $1/2$. Generalization is mathematically impossible without <strong>inductive bias</strong> — a structural commitment about the generating process. The rest of this treatise traces how AI has encoded inductive bias, where statistical inductive biases break down, and how Hyperactivity makes structural invariants explicit inside a checked type theory.</p>

  <h3 id="sec-1-5">1.5 Linear Models, Gauss–Markov, Generative vs. Discriminative Classifiers, and Unsupervised Geometry</h3>
  <p>Before neural networks, every practitioner must master the exact linear and geometric backbone of machine learning — where closed-form linear algebra exposes every statistical trade-off without hiding behind black-box optimization.</p>

  <p><strong>Ordinary Least Squares, Pseudoinverse, and the Gauss–Markov Theorem.</strong> Given a design matrix $X \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times p}$ with thin SVD $X = U \Sigma V^\top$ and targets $y \in \mathbb{R}^n$, minimizing $\|X w - y\|_2^2$ yields the normal equations $X^\top X w = X^\top y$. When $X$ has full column rank, $\hat{w}_{\mathrm{OLS}} = (X^\top X)^{-1} X^\top y = V \Sigma^{-1} U^\top y$, and the fitted values $\hat{y} = X \hat{w}_{\mathrm{OLS}} = U U^\top y$ are the orthogonal projection of $y$ onto $\mathrm{col}(X)$. When $X$ is rank-deficient or overparameterized ($p \gt n$), infinitely many minimizers exist; the unique minimum-$\ell_2$-norm solution is the Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse $\hat{w}_0 = X^+ y = V \Sigma^+ U^\top y$, which is also the exact limit of ridge regression $\hat{w}_\lambda = (X^\top X + \lambda I)^{-1} X^\top y$ as $\lambda \to 0^+$ and the exact convergence point of gradient descent initialized at $0$. Under homoscedastic uncorrelated errors ($\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon]=0, \mathrm{Cov}(\varepsilon)=\sigma^2 I_n$), the <strong>Gauss–Markov Theorem</strong> proves that $\hat{w}_{\mathrm{OLS}}$ has the smallest covariance matrix (in Loewner order) among all <em>linear unbiased</em> estimators — which is precisely why James–Stein and ridge regression beat it only by accepting controlled bias!</p>

  <p><strong>Generative vs. Discriminative Classification (Ng &amp; Jordan, 2002).</strong> A <em>generative</em> classifier models the joint law $P(X, Y) = P(Y) P(X \mid Y)$ and applies Bayes' rule; a <em>discriminative</em> classifier models $P(Y \mid X)$ (or the decision boundary) directly. Consider Gaussian class-conditionals $X \mid (Y = c) \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_c, \Sigma_c)$ with class priors $\pi_c$. Computing the log-posterior ratio gives:</p>
  <div class="eq-box">$$\log\frac{P(Y = 1 \mid x)}{P(Y = 0 \mid x)} \;=\; -\frac{1}{2}(x - \mu_1)^\top \Sigma_1^{-1}(x - \mu_1) + \frac{1}{2}(x - \mu_0)^\top \Sigma_0^{-1}(x - \mu_0) + \frac{1}{2}\log\frac{|\Sigma_0|}{|\Sigma_1|} + \log\frac{\pi_1}{\pi_0}.$$</div>
  <p>With distinct covariances $\Sigma_1 \ne \Sigma_0$, the decision surface is a quadratic hypersurface (<strong>Quadratic Discriminant Analysis, QDA</strong>). When classes share a common covariance $\Sigma_1 = \Sigma_0 = \Sigma$, the quadratic terms $x^\top \Sigma^{-1} x$ cancel identically, collapsing the log-odds to a linear function $w^\top x + b$ with $w = \Sigma^{-1}(\mu_1 - \mu_0)$ — <strong>Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA)</strong> (Fisher, 1936). <strong>Logistic Regression</strong> fits that exact same linear log-odds form $P(Y=1 \mid x) = \sigma(w^\top x + b)$ directly by maximizing conditional likelihood, without assuming $X \mid Y$ is Gaussian. Ng &amp; Jordan (2002) proved the classic two-regime contrast: Naive Bayes / LDA converges to its asymptotic error at rate $O(\log p / n)$ (faster in the small-sample regime), whereas logistic regression needs $O(p / n)$ samples yet achieves strictly lower asymptotic error whenever the generative Gaussian/independence assumption is misspecified.</p>

  <p><strong>Unsupervised Learning: PCA, Eckart–Young–Mirsky, Clustering, and Spectral Geometry.</strong> Given centered data $X \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times p}$ with sample covariance $\widehat{\Sigma} = \frac{1}{n} X^\top X$, <strong>Principal Component Analysis (PCA)</strong> (Pearson, 1901; Hotelling, 1933) solves two equivalent problems: (i) finding orthonormal directions $W \in \mathbb{R}^{p \times k}$ maximizing projected variance $\mathrm{tr}(W^\top \widehat{\Sigma} W)$, and (ii) finding the rank-$k$ orthogonal projection minimizing Frobenius reconstruction error $\|X - X W W^\top\|_F^2$. By the <strong>Eckart–Young–Mirsky Theorem</strong> (1936), both are solved identically by the top $k$ right singular vectors of $X$ (equivalently eigenvectors of $\widehat{\Sigma}$). Replacing dot products with a kernel matrix $K$ yields <strong>Kernel PCA</strong> (Schölkopf et al., 1998). For non-convex cluster geometries where Lloyd's <strong>$k$-means</strong> (1982; initialized via $k$-means++, Arthur &amp; Vassilvitskii, 2007) fails because it imposes spherical Voronoi cells, <strong>Spectral Clustering</strong> (Shi &amp; Malik, 2000; Ng, Jordan &amp; Weiss, 2002) constructs a similarity graph Laplacian $L = D - W$ (whose quadratic form $f^\top L f = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{i,j} W_{ij}(f_i - f_j)^2$ measures cut smoothness) and embeds vertices via the smallest non-trivial eigenvectors (relaxing NP-hard RatioCut / NormalizedCut into a tractable eigenproblem).</p>
