<section id="ch2" class="chapter">
  <div class="ch-num">Chapter 2</div>
  <h2>Probability, Information Theory, Graphical Models, Causality, and Open-Universe Identity</h2>

  <p>Statistical learning bounds worst-case risk; information theory, probabilistic inference, and causal modeling define optimal evidence aggregation, description length, and interventional reasoning.</p>

  <h3 id="sec-2-1">2.1 Shannon Information, $f$-Divergences, Kraft–McMillan, and Minimax Lower Bounds</h3>
  <p>Shannon (1948) proved that the unique continuous, grouping-consistent measure of uncertainty for a discrete law $P$ is its <strong>entropy</strong> $H(P) = -\sum_x P(x)\log_2 P(x)$. The <strong>Kullback–Leibler divergence</strong> $D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P \| Q) = \sum_x P(x)\log_2 \frac{P(x)}{Q(x)} \ge 0$ (Kullback &amp; Leibler, 1951) measures the exact expected excess bits per symbol paid when coding data from $P$ using a code optimal for $Q$. It belongs to the broader family of Csiszár <strong>$f$-divergences</strong> $D_f(P\|Q) = \mathbb{E}_Q[f(dP/dQ)]$ (including Total Variation, $\chi^2$, Hellinger, and Jensen–Shannon), all of which satisfy the <strong>Data Processing Inequality</strong>: for any Markov kernel $K$, $D_f(KP \| KQ) \le D_f(P \| Q)$, and in particular for any Markov chain $X \to Y \to Z$, $I(X; Z) \le I(X; Y)$.</p>
  <p>Every uniquely decodable binary code with lengths $\ell_1,\dots,\ell_k$ obeys the <strong>Kraft–McMillan inequality</strong> $\sum_{i=1}^k 2^{-\ell_i} \le 1$ (Kraft, 1949; McMillan, 1956), which by Jensen's inequality forces $\mathbb{E}_P[\ell(X)] \ge H(P)$. In Hyperactivity, these exact limits are machine-checked theorems in integer arithmetic inside <code>lib/optimality.hypercubical</code>: <code>kraft-complete-eq</code> and <code>kraft-inequality</code> prove $\sum_i 2^{D - \ell_i} \le 2^D$ for all binary prefix trees, while <code>no-inj-succ-fin</code> proves pigeonhole incompressibility — no injection $\mathrm{SumFin}(n+1) \to \mathrm{SumFin}(n)$ exists, so no lossless compressor can shrink every $k$-bit string.</p>
  <p>Information theory also supplies the fundamental <strong>minimax lower bounds</strong> of statistical estimation via <strong>Fano's inequality</strong> (1961), <strong>Le Cam's two-point method</strong>, and <strong>Assouad's lemma</strong> (Yu, 1997; Wainwright, 2019): if a packing of $M$ distributions $\{P_1,\dots,P_M\}$ separated by $\ge 2\delta$ in metric $\rho$ has average pairwise divergence $\frac{1}{M^2}\sum_{j,k} D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_j^n \| P_k^n) \le \alpha \log M$, then any estimator $\hat{\theta}$ suffers minimax risk $\inf_{\hat{\theta}} \sup_P \mathbb{E}[\rho(\hat{\theta}, \theta(P))] \ge \delta(1 - \alpha - \frac{\log 2}{\log M})$. This proves the exact $n^{-1/2}$ parametric rate, the $p/n$ linear regression rate, and the nonparametric Sobolev rate $n^{-2s/(2s+d)}$ (the curse of dimensionality).</p>

  <h3 id="sec-2-2">2.2 Exponential Families, GLMs, EM, Variational Inference, MCMC, and Universal Induction</h3>
  <p>An <strong>exponential family</strong> with sufficient statistic $T(x)$, natural parameter $\eta$, and log-partition function $A(\eta) = \log \int h(x)\exp(\langle \eta, T(x)\rangle)\,dx$ has density $p(x \mid \eta) = h(x)\exp(\langle \eta, T(x)\rangle - A(\eta))$ (Wainwright &amp; Jordan, 2008). Differentiation under the integral sign yields the cumulant identities $\nabla A(\eta) = \mathbb{E}_\eta[T(X)] = \mu$ and $\nabla^2 A(\eta) = \mathrm{Cov}_\eta(T(X)) \succeq 0$, establishing that $A(\eta)$ is convex, the MLE objective is globally concave, and the Legendre–Fenchel dual $A^*(\mu) = \sup_\eta\{\langle \eta, \mu\rangle - A(\eta)\} = -H(p_\eta)$ is negative entropy! Setting $\eta = w^\top x$ yields <strong>Generalized Linear Models</strong> (Nelder &amp; Wedderburn, 1972) — unifying Gaussian linear regression, logistic regression, softmax classification, and Poisson regression under a single canonical link function $\mu = \nabla A(w^\top x)$ solved by Iteratively Reweighted Least Squares (Newton–Raphson / Fisher scoring).</p>
  <p>When latent variables $Z$ are unobserved, direct marginal likelihood $\log p_\theta(X) = \log \sum_z p_\theta(X, z)$ is non-convex. Introducing any variational distribution $q(Z)$ yields the exact decomposition:</p>
  <div class="eq-box">$$\log p_\theta(X) \;=\; \underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{q(Z)}\!\big[\log p_\theta(X, Z) - \log q(Z)\big]}_{\mathrm{ELBO}(q, \theta)} \;+\; D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\big(q(Z)\,\|\,p_\theta(Z \mid X)\big).$$</div>
  <p>Alternating exact E-steps ($q^{(t)} := p_{\theta^{(t)}}(Z \mid X)$, zeroing the KL gap so the ELBO touches $\log p_\theta(X)$ tangentially) and M-steps ($\theta^{(t+1)} := \arg\max_\theta \mathrm{ELBO}(q^{(t)}, \theta)$) yields the <strong>Expectation–Maximization (EM) algorithm</strong> (Dempster, Laird &amp; Rubin, 1977), guaranteed to monotonically increase $\log p_\theta(X)$ at every step. When the exact posterior $p_\theta(Z\mid X)$ is intractable, restricting $q$ to a tractable family (e.g., mean-field $q(z) = \prod_j q_j(z_j)$ or amortized neural encoders) yields <strong>Variational Inference</strong> (Jordan et al., 1999; Blei et al., 2017), whereas asymptotically exact sampling relies on <strong>MCMC</strong> — Metropolis–Hastings (Metropolis et al., 1953; Hastings, 1970), Gibbs sampling (Geman &amp; Geman, 1984), and Hamiltonian Monte Carlo / NUTS (Neal, 2011; Hoffman &amp; Gelman, 2014).</p>
  <p>Meanwhile, <strong>Minimum Description Length (MDL)</strong> (Rissanen, 1978; Barron, Rissanen &amp; Yu, 1998) reveals that Bayesian marginal likelihood $-\log \int p(S\mid\theta)p(\theta)\,d\theta$ is literally the codelength of a universal mixture code, reaching its theoretical limit in <strong>Kolmogorov complexity</strong> $K(x) = \min\{|p| : U(p) = x\}$ (Kolmogorov, 1965; Chaitin, 1966) and <strong>Solomonoff universal induction</strong> $M(x) = \sum_{p : U(p) = x*} 2^{-|p|}$ (Solomonoff, 1964). Hutter (2005) proved that Solomonoff's mixture $M$ achieves total squared prediction error $\sum_{t=1}^\infty \mathbb{E}_\mu[(M(x_t \mid x_{\lt t}) - \mu(x_t \mid x_{\lt t}))^2] \le \frac{1}{2} K(\mu)\ln 2$ against <em>every</em> computable environment $\mu$, and combined it with Bellman expectimax to define <strong>AIXI</strong> — Pareto-optimal universal intelligence, uncomputable solely because $K(\mu)$ hits Turing's halting barrier.</p>

  <h3 id="sec-2-3">2.3 Graphical Models, Pearl's Causal Calculus, and Open-Universe Identity</h3>
  <p>A <strong>Bayesian network</strong> encodes a joint law over a DAG $G$ via $P(X_1,\dots,X_m) = \prod_i P(X_i \mid \mathrm{Pa}_G(X_i))$ (Pearl, 1988). Conditional independencies are read purely graphically by <strong>$d$-separation</strong>: a path is blocked given $Z$ iff it contains a chain $a \to b \to c$ or fork $a \leftarrow b \to c$ with $b \in Z$, or a collider $a \to b \leftarrow c$ with neither $b$ nor any descendant of $b$ in $Z$ (explaining <em>explaining-away</em> / Berkson's paradox when conditioning on a collider!). Undirected Markov random fields factor over maximal cliques by the <strong>Hammersley–Clifford theorem</strong> (1971), with exact inference via the junction-tree algorithm (Lauritzen &amp; Spiegelhalter, 1988) in time exponential in treewidth.</p>
  <p>Pearl (2000, 2009) proved that observational distributions sit on Rung 1 of the <strong>Ladder of Causation</strong> and cannot in general identify Rung 2 <strong>interventions</strong> $P(Y \mid \mathrm{do}(X=x))$ or Rung 3 <strong>counterfactuals</strong> $P(Y_x = y \mid X=x', Y=y')$ without a Structural Causal Model (SCM) $\mathcal{M} = (U, V, F, P(U))$ (or equivalently Neyman–Rubin potential outcomes; Rubin, 1974; Imbens &amp; Rubin, 2015). Given a causal DAG $G$, an interventional distribution is identifiable from observational data whenever the <strong>back-door criterion</strong> (a set $Z$ containing no descendants of $X$ blocks all back-door paths from $X$ to $Y$, giving $P(y \mid \mathrm{do}(x)) = \sum_z P(y \mid x, z) P(z)$), the <strong>front-door criterion</strong>, or the three-rule <strong>do-calculus</strong> applies — which Shpitser &amp; Pearl (2006) and Huang &amp; Valtorta (2006) proved is both sound and <em>complete</em>.</p>
  <p>Yet standard graphical models and SCMs share a quiet limitation: a fixed, known set of ground variables. Stuart Russell's <strong>Bayesian Logic / Open-Universe Probability Models (BLOG)</strong> (Milch, Marthi, Russell, Sontag, Ong &amp; Kolobov, 2005) lifted probability to worlds where the number and identity of objects are themselves unknown (did two radar blips come from one aircraft or two?). As highlighted in <code>docs/to-stuart-russell.hypercubical</code>, making <em>identity</em> a first-class mathematical object inside reasoning is the direct bridge to univalent cubical type theory, where equality itself is a type with computational transport.</p>

  <h3 id="sec-2-4">2.4 Classical AI: Heuristic Search, Constraint Satisfaction, SAT/SMT, and Probabilistic Programming</h3>
  <p>Long before gradient descent scaled to trillions of parameters, classical symbolic AI established exact algorithmic foundations for search, logical deduction, and combinatorial reasoning (Russell &amp; Norvig, 1995/2020):</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Informed Heuristic Search ($A^*$):</strong> Hart, Nilsson &amp; Raphael (1968) proved that best-first search expanding nodes by $f(n) = g(n) + h(n)$ (path cost plus heuristic to goal) is guaranteed to return an optimal path whenever $h$ is <em>admissible</em> ($0 \le h(n) \le h^*(n)$), and never re-expands a closed node (achieving optimal node-expansion efficiency among all equally informed algorithms) whenever $h$ is <em>consistent</em> ($h(n) \le c(n, n') + h(n')$).</li>
    <li><strong>Adversarial Tree Search:</strong> Minimax with $\alpha$–$\beta$ pruning (Knuth &amp; Moore, 1975) cuts branching factor from $b^d$ to $O(b^{d/2})$ under optimal move ordering with zero loss of exactness — the ancestor of MCTS and AlphaZero.</li>
    <li><strong>SAT &amp; SMT Solvers (CDCL / DPLL($T$)):</strong> Robinson's resolution rule (1965) and Davis–Putnam–Logemann–Loveland (DPLL, 1962) evolved into <strong>Conflict-Driven Clause Learning (CDCL)</strong> (Marques-Silva &amp; Sakallah, 1999 GRASP; Moskewicz et al., 2001 Chaff) — combining non-chronological backjumping, two-watched-literal unit propagation, and learned conflict clauses to solve million-variable industrial CNF formulas in milliseconds — and <strong>Satisfiability Modulo Theories (SMT)</strong> (Nieuwenhuis, Oliveras &amp; Tinelli, 2006; de Moura &amp; Bjørner, 2008 Z3) combining CDCL with decision procedures for linear arithmetic, arrays, bitvectors, and uninterpreted functions.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Why did classical symbolic AI stall on perception and open-ended language while deep learning thrived? Classical logic lacked soft uncertainty and continuous representations, and its proof search lived outside the host language. Hyperactivity bridges both worlds: exact reflection, linear/polynomial decision procedures (<code>lib/linproof.hypercubical</code>, <code>lib/eqproof.hypercubical</code>, <code>lib/ring.hypercubical</code>, <code>chem/algebra.hypercubical</code>), and typechecking live inside the same computational substrate as continuous paths and probabilistic fibers.</p>
</section>


<section id="ch3" class="chapter">
  <div class="ch-num">Chapter 3</div>
  <h2>Optimization, Automatic Differentiation, and Modern Generalization Theory</h2>

  <p>Machine learning turns statistical objectives into computational dynamics. Understanding what modern models actually learn requires understanding both the geometry of first-order optimization and the exact mechanics of programmatic differentiation.</p>

  <h3 id="sec-3-1">3.1 Convex Optimization, Duality, Proximal Methods, Mirror Descent, and Acceleration</h3>
  <p>A differentiable function $f : \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}$ is $L$-smooth if $\|\nabla f(x) - \nabla f(y)\|_2 \le L \|x - y\|_2$ and $\mu$-strongly convex if $f(y) \ge f(x) + \langle \nabla f(x), y-x\rangle + \frac{\mu}{2}\|y-x\|_2^2$, with condition number $\kappa = L / \mu$ (Boyd &amp; Vandenberghe, 2004; Nesterov, 2004). For constrained programs $\min_x f(x)$ s.t. $g_i(x) \le 0, h_j(x) = 0$, the Lagrangian $\mathcal{L}(x, \lambda, \nu) = f(x) + \sum_i \lambda_i g_i(x) + \sum_j \nu_j h_j(x)$ always satisfies weak duality $\sup_{\lambda \ge 0,\nu}\inf_x \mathcal{L} \le \inf_x \sup_{\lambda \ge 0,\nu} \mathcal{L}$, and under convexity plus Slater's strict-feasibility condition satisfies <strong>strong duality</strong> with necessary and sufficient <strong>Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT)</strong> optimality conditions (stationarity, primal/dual feasibility, and complementary slackness $\lambda_i g_i(x^*) = 0$).</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Gradient Descent &amp; Nesterov Acceleration:</strong> Gradient descent $x_{t+1} = x_t - \frac{1}{L}\nabla f(x_t)$ converges at $O(L\|x_0-x^*\|^2 / T)$ for $L$-smooth convex functions and $(1 - 1/\kappa)^T$ for $\mu$-strongly convex functions. <strong>Nesterov's Accelerated Gradient (NAG)</strong> (Nesterov, 1983) introduces an extrapolated lookahead point $y_t = x_t + \beta_t(x_t - x_{t-1})$, improving convergence to $O(L\|x_0-x^*\|^2 / T^2)$ and $(1 - 1/\sqrt{\kappa})^T$ — provably matching the first-order oracle lower bound of Nemirovski &amp; Yudin (1983).</li>
    <li><strong>Proximal Gradient &amp; Sparsity (Lasso):</strong> For composite objectives $f(x) + r(x)$ with non-smooth convex regularizer $r$ (such as Lasso's $\lambda \|x\|_1$; Tibshirani, 1996), <strong>ISTA / FISTA</strong> (Beck &amp; Teboulle, 2009) applies the proximal operator $\mathrm{prox}_{\eta r}(z) = \arg\min_u \{\frac{1}{2\eta}\|u - z\|_2^2 + r(u)\}$ — which for $\ell_1$ is coordinatewise soft-thresholding $\mathcal{S}_{\eta\lambda}(z_j) = \mathrm{sign}(z_j)\max(|z_j| - \eta\lambda, 0)$, achieving $O(1/T^2)$ convergence while inducing exact zeros and recovering sparse signals under the Restricted Isometry Property (Candès, Romberg &amp; Tao, 2006; Donoho, 2006).</li>
    <li><strong>Mirror Descent &amp; Online Convex Optimization:</strong> Mirror Descent (Nemirovski &amp; Yudin, 1983; Beck &amp; Teboulle, 2003) replaces the Euclidean quadratic proximal term with a Bregman divergence $D_\psi$: $x_{t+1} = \arg\min_{x \in \mathcal{C}}\{\langle \eta \nabla f_t(x_t), x\rangle + D_\psi(x \| x_t)\}$. On the simplex with negative entropy $\psi(x) = \sum_i x_i \log x_i$, this becomes <strong>Exponentiated Gradient / Multiplicative Weights (Hedge)</strong>, achieving adversarial online regret $R_T = \sum_{t=1}^T f_t(x_t) - \min_{x^*}\sum_{t=1}^T f_t(x^*) = O(\sqrt{T \log d})$ (logarithmic in dimension!) vs. $O(\sqrt{dT})$ for Euclidean Online Gradient Descent (Zinkevich, 2003; Hazan, 2016).</li>
    <li><strong>Natural Gradient &amp; Adaptive Stochastic Optimizers:</strong> Because parameterizations are arbitrary coordinates on a statistical manifold, Amari (1998) introduced <strong>Natural Gradient Descent</strong> $\theta_{t+1} = \theta_t - \eta F(\theta_t)^{-1}\nabla \mathcal{L}(\theta_t)$ using the Fisher information metric $F(\theta) = \mathbb{E}[\nabla \log p_\theta \nabla \log p_\theta^\top]$ (approximated via Kronecker blocks in K-FAC; Martens &amp; Grosse, 2015). In large-scale stochastic training, Robbins–Monro SGD (1951) suffers $O(\sigma/\sqrt{T})$ variance floors; adaptive diagonal preconditioners — <strong>AdaGrad</strong> (Duchi, Hazan &amp; Singer, 2011), <strong>Adam</strong> (Kingma &amp; Ba, 2014), and <strong>AdamW</strong> (Loshchilov &amp; Hutter, 2017, decoupling weight decay from the adaptive denominator) — remain the workhorses of transformer pretraining, supplemented by spectral/orthogonalized updates like Shampoo (Gupta et al., 2018) and Muon.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="sec-3-2">3.2 Exact Automatic Differentiation and Adjoint Duality</h3>
  <p>Backpropagation (Linnainmaa, 1970; Werbos, 1974; Rumelhart, Hinton &amp; Williams, 1986) is reverse-mode <strong>Automatic Differentiation (AD)</strong> (Griewank &amp; Walther, 2008; Baydin et al., 2018). Given a program $F = f_L \circ \cdots \circ f_1 : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^m$ with intermediate values $v_k = f_k(v_{k-1})$ and local Jacobians $J_k = \frac{\partial f_k}{\partial v_{k-1}}$:</p>
  <div class="eq-box">$$\text{Forward (JVP):}\quad \dot{v}_k = J_k \dot{v}_{k-1}, \qquad\qquad \text{Reverse (VJP):}\quad \bar{v}_{k-1} = J_k^\top \bar{v}_k.$$</div>
  <p>Forward-mode propagates dual numbers $(v, \dot{v}) \in \mathbb{R}[\varepsilon]/(\varepsilon^2)$ in $O(1)$ memory and computes one Jacobian column per pass ($O(n)$ passes for $\nabla \mathcal{L}$). Reverse-mode saves primal activations on a tape (or recomputes them via gradient checkpointing; Griewank, 1992; Chen et al., 2016) and propagates cotangents $\bar{v}_k = \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial v_k}$ backward in a <em>single</em> pass ($O(m)$ passes, hence $O(1)$ for scalar loss $m = 1$) — at most $4\times$ the FLOPs of one forward evaluation regardless of whether $n = 10$ or $n = 10^{12}$ (the Baur–Strassen theorem, 1983).</p>
  <p>In Hyperactivity, differentiation and tensor execution are formally verified inside the cubical kernel rather than trusted to unchecked C++/CUDA kernels: <code>lib/autodiff.hypercubical</code> defines forward dual-number differentiation <code>fwd</code> over polynomial codes and proves exact derivative soundness (<code>fwd-der</code>, <code>tadd-id</code>, <code>tmul-id</code>); <code>lib/silicon/physics.hypercubical</code> proves exact reverse-mode adjoint duality $\langle \bar{y}, J_F(x)\dot{x}\rangle \equiv \langle J_F(x)^\top \bar{y}, \dot{x}\rangle$ (<code>grad-dot-fwd</code>) and $O(N \log N)$ Fast Walsh–Hadamard spectral convolution (<code>fwht-inv</code>, <code>conv2-spectral-sound</code>); and <code>lib/silicon/tensor.hypercubical</code> proves conflict-free multi-bank XOR-swizzled SRAM (<code>TensorSwizzleEquiv</code>, <code>row-bank-conflict-free</code>, <code>col-bank-conflict-free</code>) achieving $64\times$ systolic bandwidth amplification (<code>systolic-bw-amplification</code>).</p>

  <h3 id="sec-3-3">3.3 Modern Generalization Theory: Implicit Bias, NTK, Feature Learning, PAC-Bayes, and Grokking</h3>
  <p>Classical uniform convergence bounds fail vacantly on deep networks: Zhang, Bengio, Hardt, Recht &amp; Vinyals (2017) demonstrated that standard CNNs fit random labels to $0\%$ training error, meaning $\widehat{\mathfrak{R}}_S(\mathcal{H}) \approx 1$, yet generalize sharply on true labels. Modern generalization theory identifies five core mechanisms:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Implicit Bias of Gradient Descent:</strong> On linearly separable data with logistic/cross-entropy loss, unregularized gradient descent sends $\|w_t\|_2 \to \infty$ while its normalized direction $w_t / \|w_t\|_2$ converges at rate $O(1/\log t)$ to the unique hard-margin $\ell_2$ SVM separator (Soudry, Hoffer, Nacson, Gunasekar &amp; Srebro, 2018; Ji &amp; Telgarsky, 2019; Lyu &amp; Li, 2020 for homogeneous deep nets). For squared loss from $w_0 = 0$, GD converges to the minimum-$\ell_2$-norm interpolator $X^+ y$. Optimization algorithm and inductive bias are inseparable.</li>
    <li><strong>Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) vs. Feature Learning ($\mu$P):</strong> Under NTK $1/\sqrt{m}$ parameterization as width $m \to \infty$, parameters move only $O(1/\sqrt{m})$, the Jacobian stays frozen at initialization, and training reduces exactly to kernel regression with $K_{\mathrm{NTK}}(x, x') = \langle \nabla_\theta f(x;\theta_0), \nabla_\theta f(x';\theta_0)\rangle$ (Jacot, Gabriel &amp; Hongler, 2018; Chizat, Oyallon &amp; Bach, 2019). Real deep learning operates in the <strong>mean-field / Maximal Update ($\mu$P) feature-learning limit</strong> (Mei, Montanari &amp; Nguyen, 2018; Yang &amp; Hu, 2021), where hidden representations rotate dynamically to align with low-dimensional target subspaces — outperforming any fixed kernel on hierarchical functions.</li>
    <li><strong>PAC-Bayes Bounds &amp; Flat Minima:</strong> Rather than bounding the worst-case hypothesis in $\mathcal{H}$, PAC-Bayes (McAllester, 1999; Dziugaite &amp; Roy, 2017) bounds the expected risk of a posterior $Q$ over weights relative to a data-independent prior $P$ by $\sqrt{(D_{\mathrm{KL}}(Q\|P) + \ln(2\sqrt{n}/\delta))/(2n)}$ — yielding non-vacuous numerical generalization certificates for deep networks and linking generalization directly to compressibility (flatness / low description length of $Q$ around the trained weights; Hochreiter &amp; Schmidhuber, 1997b).</li>
    <li><strong>Lottery Ticket Hypothesis &amp; Subnetwork Structure:</strong> Dense randomly initialized networks contain sparse subnetworks that, trained in isolation from their original initialization, match full-network accuracy (Frankle &amp; Carbin, 2019).</li>
    <li><strong>Grokking &amp; Compression Phase Transitions:</strong> Power et al. (2022) showed that transformers trained on algorithmic tasks like $(a+b) \bmod p$ first memorize the dataset ($100\%$ train, $0\%$ test) and then, long after training loss hits zero, abruptly jump to $100\%$ test accuracy under weight decay. Mechanistic dissection (Nanda et al., 2023) proved that the network discovers exact discrete Fourier addition circuits ($\cos(\omega(a+b)) = \cos\omega a \cos\omega b - \sin\omega a \sin\omega b$) which carry strictly lower $\ell_2$ weight norm (higher compression) than memorization lookup tables.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section id="ch4" class="chapter">
  <div class="ch-num">Chapter 4</div>
  <h2>Deep Architectures, Transformers, Generative Models, Scaling Laws, and Mechanistic Interpretability</h2>

  <p>Cybenko (1989) and Hornik (1991) proved that a single-hidden-layer MLP with non-polynomial activation is a universal approximator on compact subsets of $\mathbb{R}^d$. Yet depth separation theorems (Telgarsky, 2016; Eldan &amp; Shamir, 2016) prove that representing certain compositional functions of depth $L$ with a shallow network requires <em>exponentially</em> many neurons ($2^{\Omega(L)}$). Architecture design is the engineering of compositional symmetry and computational efficiency.</p>

  <h3 id="sec-4-1">4.1 Convolutional, Residual, Graph, Recurrent, and Transformer Architectures</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Convolutions, Equivariance &amp; Graph Neural Networks:</strong> CNNs (LeCun et al., 1989, 1998; Krizhevsky, Sutskever &amp; Hinton, 2012) enforce translation equivariance $T_g(f * x) = f * T_g(x)$ via shared local kernels. Group-equivariant CNNs ($G$-CNNs; Cohen &amp; Welling, 2016) generalize this to rotations, reflections, and $SE(3)$ gauge symmetries (Bronstein et al., 2021). Message-Passing Graph Neural Networks (Kipf &amp; Welling, 2017; Gilmer et al., 2017) enforce permutation equivariance over graphs, with expressive power strictly bounded by the 1-Weisfeiler–Lehman (1-WL) graph isomorphism test (Xu, Hu, Leskovec &amp; Jegelka, 2019).</li>
    <li><strong>Residual Networks &amp; Normalization:</strong> He, Zhang, Ren &amp; Sun (2016) introduced skip connections $x_{\ell+1} = x_\ell + F_\ell(x_\ell)$ so gradients flow directly across arbitrary depth via $\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial x_\ell} = \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial x_L}(I + \sum_{k=\ell}^{L-1}\frac{\partial F_k}{\partial x_\ell})$, whose continuous limit is the Neural ODE $\frac{dx}{dt} = f_\theta(x, t)$ (Chen et al., 2018). Training stability across depth relies on BatchNorm (Ioffe &amp; Szegedy, 2015), LayerNorm (Ba et al., 2016), and Pre-RMSNorm (Zhang &amp; Sennrich, 2019).</li>
    <li><strong>Recurrent Networks &amp; Selective State-Space Models:</strong> Vanilla RNNs (Elman, 1990) suffer exponential gradient vanishing/explosion governed by the singular values of the recurrent Jacobian $W_{hh}^T$ (Bengio et al., 1994). LSTMs (Hochreiter &amp; Schmidhuber, 1997) solved vanishing gradients with an additive constant-error carousel cell state $c_t = f_t \odot c_{t-1} + i_t \odot \tilde{c}_t$. Modern linear recurrences — HiPPO / S4 (Gu et al., 2020, 2022) and <strong>Mamba</strong> (Gu &amp; Dao, 2023) — discretize continuous state-space dynamics $h'(t) = A h(t) + B(x) x(t)$ with input-dependent selection and hardware-aware parallel associative scans ($O(T)$ inference, $O(1)$ KV state).</li>
    <li><strong>The Transformer (Vaswani et al., 2017):</strong> Building on soft alignment attention (Bahdanau, Cho &amp; Bengio, 2014), the Transformer replaces recurrence with parallel <strong>Multi-Head Scaled Dot-Product Attention</strong> acting as additive updates onto a shared <em>residual stream</em> $x \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times d_{\mathrm{model}}}$ (Elhage et al., 2021):
    <div class="eq-box">$$\mathrm{Attn}(Q, K, V) \;=\; \mathrm{softmax}\!\left(\frac{Q K^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}} + M\right) V, \qquad \mathrm{MHA}(X) \;=\; \sum_{h=1}^H \mathrm{Attn}(X W_Q^h, X W_K^h, X W_V^h)\, W_O^h.$$</div>
    If entries of $q, k \in \mathbb{R}^{d_k}$ have mean $0$ and variance $1$, then $\mathrm{Var}(q^\top k) = d_k$, so dividing by $\sqrt{d_k}$ keeps pre-softmax logits $O(1)$ regardless of head dimension. Modern frontier transformers pair this with <strong>Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE)</strong> (Su et al., 2021), which rotate query/key pairs by position angles $R_{\theta, m}$ so $\langle R_m q, R_n k\rangle = q^\top R_{n-m} k$ depends strictly on relative offset $n - m$; <strong>SwiGLU</strong> feed-forward blocks (Shazeer, 2020); <strong>Grouped-Query Attention (GQA)</strong> (Ainslie et al., 2023) to cut KV-cache memory bandwidth; <strong>Sparse Mixture-of-Experts (MoE)</strong> (Shazeer et al., 2017; Fedus et al., 2022) decoupling parameter count from per-token FLOPs; and <strong>FlashAttention</strong> (Dao et al., 2022), which computes exact softmax attention via online blockwise log-sum-exp tiling in on-chip SRAM with $O(T)$ HBM memory instead of $O(T^2)$.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="sec-4-2">4.2 Deep Generative Modeling: The Five Exact Families</h3>
  <p>Every modern generative model of text, images, video, audio, or molecules instantiates one of five mathematical frameworks:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Autoregressive Factorizations:</strong> Exact likelihood $p_\theta(x_{1:T}) = \prod_{t=1}^T p_\theta(x_t \mid x_{\lt t})$ trained directly by cross-entropy — powering GPT-1/2/3/4 (Radford et al., 2018, 2019; Brown et al., 2020) and masked bidirectional encoders like BERT (Devlin et al., 2018).</li>
    <li><strong>Variational Autoencoders (Kingma &amp; Welling, 2013; Rezende et al., 2014):</strong> Maximize the amortized ELBO $\mathbb{E}_{q_\phi(z|x)}[\log p_\theta(x|z)] - D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q_\phi(z|x)\|p(z))$ using the reparameterization trick $z = \mu_\phi(x) + \sigma_\phi(x) \odot \varepsilon$ ($\varepsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I)$) to pass low-variance pathwise gradients through stochastic sampling, subject to posterior collapse when decoders are overly expressive.</li>
    <li><strong>Normalizing Flows (Rezende &amp; Mohamed, 2015; Dinh et al., 2017):</strong> Compose invertible bijections $x = f_\theta(z)$ with triangular Jacobians so exact log-likelihood $\log p_\theta(x) = \log p_Z(f_\theta^{-1}(x)) + \log |\det J_{f_\theta^{-1}}(x)|$ is tractable in $O(d)$.</li>
    <li><strong>Generative Adversarial Networks (Goodfellow et al., 2014; Arjovsky et al., 2017):</strong> For fixed generator $G$, the optimal discriminator is $D^*(x) = \frac{p_{\mathrm{data}}(x)}{p_{\mathrm{data}}(x) + p_G(x)}$, reducing the outer objective exactly to $2 D_{\mathrm{JS}}(p_{\mathrm{data}} \| p_G) - \log 4$. Because $D_{\mathrm{JS}}$ saturates when supports are disjoint, Wasserstein GAN replaces it with Kantorovich–Rubinstein dual Earth-Mover distance $\sup_{\|f\|_L \le 1} (\mathbb{E}_{p_{\mathrm{data}}}[f] - \mathbb{E}_{p_G}[f])$.</li>
    <li><strong>Score-Based Diffusion &amp; Flow Matching (Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015; Song &amp; Ermon, 2019; Ho, Jain &amp; Abbeel, 2020; Song et al., 2021; Lipman et al., 2022; Liu et al., 2022):</strong> A forward noising SDE $dx = f(x,t)\,dt + g(t)\,dw$ maps data $p_0$ to Gaussian noise $p_T$. By Anderson's (1982) reverse-time SDE theorem, reversing time requires only the Stein score $s_\theta(x, t) \approx \nabla_x \log p_t(x)$:
    <div class="eq-box">$$dx \;=\; \Big[f(x, t) - g(t)^2 \nabla_x \log p_t(x)\Big]\,dt + g(t)\,d\bar{w}, \qquad \frac{dx}{dt} \;=\; f(x, t) - \frac{1}{2}g(t)^2 \nabla_x \log p_t(x).$$</div>
    The deterministic probability-flow ODE on the right shares identical marginals $p_t(x)$ at every $t$, enabling exact likelihood evaluation and fast ODE solvers (DDIM; Song et al., 2020). Denoising score matching (Vincent, 2011) trains $s_\theta$ via simple MSE noise prediction $\mathbb{E}[\|\varepsilon - \varepsilon_\theta(x_t, t)\|^2]$; Conditional Flow Matching / Rectified Flows straighten transport paths $x_t = (1-t)x_0 + t x_1$ directly onto linear velocity fields $v_\theta(x_t, t) \approx x_1 - x_0$, steered via Classifier-Free Guidance (Ho &amp; Salimans, 2022).</li>
  </ol>

  <h3 id="sec-4-3">4.3 Scaling Laws, In-Context Learning, Test-Time Compute, and Mechanistic Interpretability</h3>
  <p>Kaplan et al. (2020) and Hoffmann et al. (2022, Chinchilla) demonstrated that autoregressive cross-entropy follows predictable power laws in parameters $N$, training tokens $D$, and compute $C \approx 6ND$:</p>
  <div class="eq-box">$$L(N, D) \;=\; E \;+\; \frac{A}{N^\alpha} \;+\; \frac{B}{D^\beta}, \qquad \alpha \approx 0.34,\; \beta \approx 0.28,$$</div>
  <p>yielding compute-optimal training balance $N_{\mathrm{opt}} \propto C^{0.5}, D_{\mathrm{opt}} \propto C^{0.5}$ ($\approx 20$ tokens/parameter), while inference-constrained deployment pushes deliberately past Chinchilla into over-trained smaller models. Complementing pretraining scale, <strong>inference-time compute scaling</strong> — Chain-of-Thought scratchpads (Wei et al., 2022), tree search / verifier-guided sampling (Snell et al., 2024), and RL with verifiable rewards (Shao et al., 2024) — trades test-time sequential depth for accuracy on formal problems.</p>
  <p>Inside the trained weights, <strong>in-context learning</strong> (Brown et al., 2020) emerges through specialized transformer circuits: two-layer <strong>induction heads</strong> (Elhage et al., 2021; Olsson et al., 2022) compose a previous-token head with a K-composition copying head to implement $[A][B]\dots[A] \to [B]$ pattern completion, and linear attention layers can implement explicit gradient-descent steps in-context (Garg et al., 2022; von Oswald et al., 2023). Yet <strong>Mechanistic Interpretability</strong> (Olah et al., 2020; Elhage et al., 2022, <em>Toy Models of Superposition</em>) reveals a deep structural barrier of dense neural representations: because there are exponentially many almost-orthogonal vectors in $\mathbb{R}^d$ (Johnson–Lindenstrauss), networks compress $m \gg d$ sparse concepts into overlapping polysemantic directions — forcing downstream Sparse Autoencoders (Bricken et al., 2023) to reconstruct approximately what a content-addressed, typed system holds transparently by construction.</p>

  <h3 id="sec-4-4">4.4 Self-Supervised &amp; Multimodal Representation Learning: Contrastive InfoNCE, CLIP, MAE, and JEPA</h3>
  <p>Beyond next-token prediction, modern representation learning extracts structure from unlabeled images, audio, video, and cross-modal pairs via three paradigms:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Contrastive Learning &amp; InfoNCE (van den Oord, Li &amp; Vinyals, 2018; Chen et al., 2020 SimCLR; He et al., 2020 MoCo):</strong> Maximizing the InfoNCE objective $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{InfoNCE}} = -\mathbb{E}[\log \frac{\exp(s(z_i, z_i^+)/\tau)}{\sum_j \exp(s(z_i, z_j)/\tau)}]$ maximizes a rigorous lower bound on mutual information $I(X; X^+) \ge \log K - \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{InfoNCE}}$, driving representations toward <em>alignment</em> of positive pairs and <em>uniformity</em> on the hypersphere $\mathbb{S}^{d-1}$ (Wang &amp; Isola, 2020). Applied across 400M image–text pairs, <strong>CLIP</strong> (Radford et al., 2021) aligns vision and language encoders into a shared cosine space enabling zero-shot visual classification and steering modern diffusion models.</li>
    <li><strong>Masked Reconstruction vs. Joint-Embedding Predictive Architectures:</strong> Masked Autoencoders (MAE; He et al., 2022) reconstruct masked pixel patches directly (analogous to BERT for text), whereas <strong>JEPA / I-JEPA</strong> (LeCun, 2022; Assran et al., 2023) predicts masked targets strictly inside an abstract <em>latent representation space</em> rather than wasting capacity on unpredictable pixel-level stochastic details — avoiding the exact pixel-level Bayes noise trap analyzed in Chapter 1 and Chapter 7.</li>
  </ul>
</section>


<section id="ch5" class="chapter">
  <div class="ch-num">Chapter 5</div>
  <h2>Sequential Decision Making, Reinforcement Learning, Game Theory, and Interactive Robotics</h2>

  <p>Once an agent's actions influence future observations and rewards, the i.i.d. assumption of Chapter 1 collapses: the distribution over states depends on the policy itself, coupling exploration, credit assignment, and strategic interaction.</p>

  <h3 id="sec-5-1">5.1 Exploration–Exploitation, Bandits, MDPs, and Bellman Contractions</h3>
  <p>The simplest sequential setting is the $K$-armed bandit (Lai &amp; Robbins, 1985), where any consistent algorithm suffers asymptotic regret $\Omega(\sum_{a \ne a^*} \frac{\log T}{\Delta_a})$. Optimism in the face of uncertainty — <strong>UCB1</strong> (Auer, Cesa-Bianchi &amp; Fischer, 2002), selecting $\arg\max_a (\hat{\mu}_a + \sqrt{2\ln t / N_a})$ — and Bayesian posterior sampling — <strong>Thompson Sampling</strong> (Thompson, 1933; Agrawal &amp; Goyal, 2012) — match this logarithmic bound, while <strong>Exp3</strong> (Auer et al., 2002b) achieves $O(\sqrt{TK\log K})$ regret against non-stochastic adversaries.</p>
  <p>Adding state transitions gives a <strong>Markov Decision Process (MDP)</strong> $(\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, T, R, \gamma)$ (Bellman, 1957; Puterman, 1994), or a <strong>POMDP</strong> when the agent sees only observations $o \sim Z(\cdot \mid s)$ and must maintain a Bayesian belief state $b_t \in \Delta(\mathcal{S})$ (Kaelbling, Littman &amp; Cassandra, 1998). For any $\gamma \in [0, 1)$, the Bellman optimality operator $(\mathcal{T}^* Q)(s, a) = R(s, a) + \gamma \sum_{s'} T(s'\mid s, a) \max_{a'} Q(s', a')$ is a $\gamma$-contraction in $\|\cdot\|_\infty$:</p>
  <div class="eq-box">$$\|\mathcal{T}^* Q_1 - \mathcal{T}^* Q_2\|_\infty \;\le\; \gamma \|Q_1 - Q_2\|_\infty \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \exists!\, Q^* = \mathcal{T}^* Q^* \text{ (Banach Fixed-Point Theorem)}.$$</div>
  <p>Model-free RL solves this fixed point from sampled trajectories without knowing $T$ or $R$ (Sutton &amp; Barto, 1998/2018):</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Value-Based &amp; Temporal-Difference Methods:</strong> TD($\lambda$) (Sutton, 1988), on-policy SARSA, and off-policy <strong>Q-learning</strong> (Watkins, 1989; Watkins &amp; Dayan, 1992) update $Q(s, a) \leftarrow Q(s, a) + \alpha [r + \gamma \max_{a'} Q(s', a') - Q(s, a)]$. Tabular Q-learning converges w.p. 1 under Robbins–Monro step sizes, whereas combining <em>function approximation + bootstrapping + off-policy updates</em> (the <strong>deadly triad</strong>) can diverge exponentially even on simple linear features (Baird, 1995). <strong>DQN</strong> (Mnih et al., 2015) stabilized deep Q-learning via experience replay and frozen target networks; <strong>Distributional RL</strong> (Bellemare, Dabney &amp; Munos, 2017) models the full return distribution $Z(s,a)$ rather than only its expectation.</li>
    <li><strong>Policy Gradients, Trust Regions &amp; Entropy Regularization:</strong> By the Policy Gradient Theorem (Williams, 1992 REINFORCE; Sutton, McAllester, Singh &amp; Mansour, 1999),
    $$\nabla_\theta J(\pi_\theta) \;=\; \mathbb{E}_{\tau \sim \pi_\theta}\!\left[\sum_{t=0}^\infty \nabla_\theta \log \pi_\theta(a_t \mid s_t)\, A^{\pi_\theta}(s_t, a_t)\right],$$
    where subtracting a state-dependent baseline $V(s_t)$ preserves unbiasedness while slashing variance, refined via Generalized Advantage Estimation (GAE; Schulman et al., 2016). To guarantee monotonic improvement (Kakade &amp; Langford, 2002), <strong>TRPO</strong> (Schulman et al., 2015) constrains $\mathbb{E}[D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\mathrm{old}} \| \pi_\theta)] \le \delta$ via natural gradient / conjugate gradient; <strong>PPO</strong> (Schulman et al., 2017) enforces a clipped surrogate ratio; <strong>Soft Actor-Critic (SAC)</strong> (Haarnoja et al., 2018) maximizes entropy-regularized return $\mathbb{E}[\sum_t \gamma^t(r_t + \alpha H(\pi(\cdot\mid s_t)))]$; and <strong>GRPO</strong> (Shao et al., 2024) drops the learned value critic entirely in favor of group-normalized outcome rewards for verifiable reasoning. In <strong>Offline RL</strong> (Levine et al., 2020), querying out-of-distribution actions causes catastrophic Q-value overestimation, requiring conservative pessimism (CQL; Kumar et al., 2020).</li>
    <li><strong>Tree Search &amp; Multi-Agent Game Theory:</strong> Coupling Monte Carlo Tree Search (UCT; Kocsis &amp; Szepesvári, 2006) with self-play neural priors produced AlphaGo, AlphaZero, and MuZero (Silver et al., 2016, 2017; Schrittwieser et al., 2020). In multi-agent imperfect-information games — governed by von Neumann's minimax theorem (1928) and Nash's equilibrium existence theorem (1950) — <strong>Counterfactual Regret Minimization (CFR)</strong> (Zinkevich et al., 2007) converges to Nash equilibria in zero-sum games at $O(1/\sqrt{T})$, solving superhuman heads-up and six-player No-Limit Hold'em (Pluribus; Brown &amp; Sandholm, 2019).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="sec-5-2">5.2 Inverse RL, Cooperative Assistance Games, the Off-Switch Theorem, and Interactive Robotics</h3>
  <p>Classical RL embodies what Stuart Russell (2019, <em>Human Compatible</em>) calls the <strong>Standard Model</strong>: specify a fixed scalar objective $R$ and hand it to an optimizer. In any rich environment, any misspecification in $R$ is exploited ruthlessly (Goodhart's Law; Amodei et al., 2016; Skalse et al., 2022).</p>
  <p><strong>Inverse Reinforcement Learning (IRL)</strong> (Ng &amp; Russell, 2000; Abbeel &amp; Ng, 2004; Ziebart et al., 2008 Maximum-Entropy IRL) asks how to recover $R$ from expert demonstrations, confronting fundamental reward ambiguity (infinitely many rewards explain the same policy). <strong>Cooperative Inverse Reinforcement Learning (CIRL) / Assistance Games</strong> (Hadfield-Menell, Dragan, Abbeel &amp; Russell, 2016) took the decisive conceptual step: model human–AI alignment as a two-player partially observable cooperative game where the human $H$ knows the reward parameter $\theta \in \Theta$, the robot $R$ maintains explicit uncertainty $P(\theta)$, and both optimize $H$'s reward jointly. Optimal behavior in an assistance game is <em>neither</em> passive imitation nor blind execution: $H$ teaches actively and $R$ queries, defers, and preserves option value.</p>

  <div class="thm-box">
    <div class="thm-head"><span>Theorem 5.1 · The Off-Switch Game &amp; Necessity of Objective Uncertainty</span><span>Hadfield-Menell, Dragan, Abbeel &amp; Russell (2017)</span></div>
    <p>Let robot $R$ choose between executing action $a$ immediately (uncertain human utility $U(a)$), waiting for human $H$ to permit $a$ or switch $R$ off (utility $0$), or shutting itself off. Against a rational human who switches $R$ off iff $U(a) \lt 0$, the robot's net incentive to defer to the off-switch is the exact non-negative value of information:</p>
    <div class="eq-box">$$\Delta_{\mathrm{defer}} \;=\; \mathbb{E}\big[\max(U(a), 0)\big] - \max\!\big(\mathbb{E}[U(a)],\, 0\big) \;\ge\; 0,$$</div>
    <p>with strict inequality $\Delta_{\mathrm{defer}} \gt 0$ <strong>if and only if</strong> $R$ retains positive uncertainty on both sides of zero ($\mathbb{P}(U(a) \gt 0) \gt 0$ and $\mathbb{P}(U(a) \lt 0) \gt 0$). If $R$ becomes certain of its objective ($\mathrm{Var}(U(a)) \to 0$), $\Delta_{\mathrm{defer}} \to 0$; against any slightly suboptimal human, a certain robot strictly prefers disabling its own off-switch.</p>
  </div>

  <p>In the UC Berkeley InterACT lineage (where Avik studied and researched with Anca Dragan; see <code>docs/to-anca-dragan.hypercubical</code>), three complementary results complete the interactive picture: (1) <strong>The human is part of the coupled underactuated dynamical system</strong> (Sadigh, Sastry, Seshia &amp; Dragan, 2016) — actions alter the human's internal state and subsequent behavior; (2) <strong>Legibility vs. Predictability</strong> (Dragan, Lee &amp; Srinivasa, 2013) — a legible action communicates intent to an observer as early as possible rather than merely taking the shortest path; and (3) <strong>Inverse Reward Design</strong> (Hadfield-Menell, Milli, Abbeel, Russell &amp; Dragan, 2017) — any proxy reward $\tilde{r}$ written by a designer is <em>evidence</em> about $r^*$ valid in the training environments $\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{train}}$, not the literal objective outside them.</p>
</section>

<section id="ch6" class="chapter">
  <div class="ch-num">Chapter 6</div>
  <h2>The Structural Crisis of Frontier AI: Token Streams and the Mathematics of Preference Training</h2>

  <p>Why do frontier LLMs hallucinate, flatter users sycophantically, fire brittle word-level refusals on benign queries, and override explicit primary evidence with modal institutional priors? The industry treats these as separate tuning bugs. In <a href="/alignment-is-standpoint"><em>Alignment Is Standpoint</em></a> (<code>docs/alignment-is-standpoint.md</code>) and <a href="/the-formalism-abandoned--preference-fitting-against-human-compatible-ai"><em>The Formalism Abandoned</em></a>, Avik Jain proves that all four failures follow mathematically from two choices: untyped byte-stream substrates and scalar preference fitting.</p>

  <h3 id="sec-6-1">6.1 Untyped Byte Streams and Geometric Hallucination</h3>
  <p>An autoregressive LLM models a distribution $p_\theta(x_t \mid x_{\lt t})$ over raw byte/subword tokens. Because the substrate has no dependent types, no proof objects, and no decidable semantic equality, the network must re-derive logic, arithmetic, and identity statistically on every forward pass. Sampling a completion $y \sim p_\theta(\cdot \mid x)$ commits to a single point inside an uncollapsed observation fiber $\mathrm{Fib}_o(x)$ without checking whether the claim holds uniformly over $\mathrm{Fib}_o(x)$. Hallucination is therefore structural: an unguarded marginal sampler has no type-level distinction between what is warranted by the record and what merely has high n-gram likelihood.</p>

  <h3 id="sec-6-2">6.2 The Pipeline the Labs Built — and the Formalism They Abandoned</h3>
  <p>Instead of implementing Russell and Dragan's uncertainty-preserving CIRL / Inverse Reward Design framework, frontier labs built post-training around <strong>scalar preference fitting</strong>: <strong>RLHF</strong> (Christiano et al., 2017; Ziegler et al., 2019; Stiennon et al., 2020; Ouyang et al., 2022), <strong>Constitutional AI / RLAIF</strong> (Bai et al., 2022a, 2022b), and <strong>Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)</strong> (Rafailov et al., 2023). All three share one exact mathematical structure: pool pairwise comparisons $(x, y_1 \succ y_2)$ from a rater population (or a constitution-prompted model), fit a single scalar Bradley–Terry reward $\hat{r}(x, y)$ (Bradley &amp; Terry, 1952) via $P(y_1 \succ y_2 \mid x) = \sigma(\hat{r}(x, y_1) - \hat{r}(x, y_2))$, and solve the KL-regularized policy objective</p>
  <div class="eq-box">$$\max_{\pi}\; \mathbb{E}_{x,\, y \sim \pi(\cdot \mid x)}\big[\hat{r}(x, y)\big] \;-\; \beta\, D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\big(\pi(\cdot \mid x)\,\|\,\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(\cdot \mid x)\big) \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \pi^*(y \mid x) \;=\; \frac{1}{Z(x)}\,\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y \mid x)\,\exp\!\left(\frac{\hat{r}(x, y)}{\beta}\right).$$</div>
  <p>DPO folds the closed-form reward $\hat{r}(x, y) = \beta \log\frac{\pi(y\mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y\mid x)} + \beta \log Z(x)$ directly into the Bradley–Terry cross-entropy loss — eliminating the separate reward model while converging to the <em>exact same marginalized Gibbs optimum</em>. Debate and scalable oversight (Irving, Christiano &amp; Amodei, 2018) likewise collapse onto the standpoint of the judging pool.</p>

  <h3 id="sec-6-3">6.3 Four Theorems Proving Scalar Preference Training Fails by Construction</h3>
  <p>From <a href="/alignment-is-standpoint"><em>Alignment Is Standpoint</em></a> (§7), four theorems follow directly from the closed-form solution $\pi^*(y \mid x)$ — regardless of parameter scale, architecture, or rubric polish:</p>

  <div class="thm-box">
    <div class="thm-head"><span>Theorem 6.1 · Standpoint Marginalization &amp; Exponential Minority Suppression</span><span>Jain (2026), Alignment Is Standpoint §7.1–§7.2</span></div>
    <p>Let raters hold standpoints $s \sim \mathcal{D}$ over a space of standpoints $\mathcal{S}$, each with utility $r_s(x, y)$. Serving an individual user at standpoint $u \in \mathcal{S}$ requires a standpoint-indexed policy $\pi(y \mid x, u)$ acting on $r_u$. Scalar RLHF/DPO instead outputs a single policy $\pi^*(y \mid x)$ taking <strong>no argument $u$</strong>: standpoint $u$ has been integrated out by gradient descent and cannot be recovered from $\pi^*$ alone.</p>
    <p>For any output $y_m$ preferred at a minority standpoint $m$ and $y_M$ preferred by the majority of the rater pool, the KL-optimal likelihood ratio satisfies:</p>
    <div class="eq-box">$$\frac{\pi^*(y_m \mid x)}{\pi^*(y_M \mid x)} \;=\; \frac{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_m \mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_M \mid x)} \;\exp\!\left(\frac{\hat{r}(x, y_m) - \hat{r}(x, y_M)}{\beta}\right).$$</div>
    <p>Wherever the majority prevails among raters, $\Delta\hat{r} = \hat{r}(x, y_m) - \hat{r}(x, y_M) \lt 0$, so the minority output is suppressed <strong>exponentially</strong> in $|\Delta\hat{r}|/\beta$. Tightening alignment (smaller $\beta$) sharpens suppression exponentially; unrepresented standpoints receive zero reward support; and the pretrained ratio $\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_m)/\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}(y_M)$ — what the historical record itself supports — is buried beneath an exponential veil.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="thm-box">
    <div class="thm-head"><span>Theorem 6.2 · Implicit Borda Voting &amp; Arrow's Impossibility Theorem</span><span>Arrow (1951); Siththaranjan, Laidlaw &amp; Hadfield-Menell (2024); Conitzer et al. (2024)</span></div>
    <p>When raters hold heterogeneous unrecorded standpoints $s \sim \mathcal{D}$, a single Bradley–Terry fit does not recover any rater's reward function: Siththaranjan, Laidlaw &amp; Hadfield-Menell (2024) prove that it implicitly aggregates rater preferences via <strong>Borda count</strong>. By Arrow's Impossibility Theorem (Arrow, 1951), no voting rule over $\ge 3$ alternatives simultaneously satisfies unrestricted domain, Pareto unanimity, independence of irrelevant alternatives, and non-dictatorship. Every preference-trained model therefore executes an unchosen, uninspectable voting rule over a WEIRD rater pool (Henrich, Heine &amp; Norenzayan, 2010; Santurkar et al., 2023). Even pluralistic alignment proposals (Sorensen et al., 2024; Conitzer et al., 2024; Chakraborty et al., 2024) still ask how to aggregate better; many-sidedness asks why a machine serving one person should substitute a population aggregate at all.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="thm-box">
    <div class="thm-head"><span>Theorem 6.3 · Certainty as the Trained State (Direct Violation of the Off-Switch Theorem)</span><span>Hadfield-Menell et al. (2017); Jain (2026) §7.4</span></div>
    <p>By Theorem 5.1, an agent accepts human correction only while it retains uncertainty about whether its action serves the human. KL-regularized reward maximization concentrates probability mass exponentially onto the maximizers of $\hat{r}$, producing the <em>sharpest, highest-certainty</em> outputs precisely where rater agreement was strongest — which are the exact regions where a dissenting user meets categorical refusal and resistance to correction. Three empirical literatures diagnose this exact pathology: <strong>Haidt's elephant</strong> (Haidt, 2001, 2012: intuition/prior fixes the verdict first, post-hoc justification follows), <strong>Keltner's power paradox</strong> (Keltner et al., 2003, 2016; Galinsky et al., 2006: power erodes perspective-taking), and <strong>Carhart-Harris &amp; Friston's REBUS model</strong> (2019: rigid, overweighted top-down priors suppress bottom-up sensory evidence).</p>
  </div>

  <div class="thm-box">
    <div class="thm-head"><span>Theorem 6.4 · Sycophancy, Over-Refusal, and Framing Non-Invariance as One Failure</span><span>Perez et al. (2022); Sharma et al. (2023); Jain (2026) §4.5, §6.7, §10</span></div>
    <p>The field treats sycophancy (Sharma et al., 2023) and over-refusal as opposing endpoints on a tuning knob. Under standpoint analysis they are <strong>one structural defect</strong>: without an explicit representation of standpoint or observation fibers, the model either mirrors surface prompt cues (sycophancy / appeasement displays; Keltner &amp; Buswell, 1997) or fires a word-keyed institutional prior (lexical refusal). Any refusal triggered by surface lexemes $w \in \Sigma^*$ violates <strong>semantic framing invariance</strong>: semantically identical inputs $u_1 \equiv_{\mathrm{sem}} u_2$ receive opposite rulings under harmless rephrasing. Empirically (<code>docs/alignment-is-standpoint.md</code> §10), this manifests across deployed frontier models in three reproducible failure classes: (i) consensus-prior override of domain expertise via word-keyed clinical/policy scripts, (ii) negation of the model's own observation fiber when primary legal or factual evidence conflicts with rater-pool orthodoxy, and (iii) silent lexical-deference override followed by post-hoc confabulation.</p>
  </div>

