  <h3 id="sec-7-2">7.2 Chollet's Definition of Intelligence: Skill-Acquisition Efficiency and Two Kinds of Abstraction</h3>
  <p>Evaluating AI on static benchmark accuracy confuses <em>memorized skill</em> with <em>intelligence</em>. François Chollet (2019, <em>On the Measure of Intelligence</em>; see <code>docs/to-francois-chollet.hypercubical</code>) gave the field's cleanest operational definition: intelligence is <strong>skill-acquisition efficiency</strong> over a scope of tasks, explicitly normalized by the system's <em>priors</em> and <em>experience</em>. Any benchmark that fails to charge a system for trillions of pretraining tokens and hardcoded task templates measures crystallized lookup, not general intelligence.</p>
  <p>Chollet's <strong>Kaleidoscope Hypothesis</strong> observes that the unbounded apparent novelty of the world is generated by recombining a compact set of recurring structural invariants, and distinguishes two fundamentally different modes of abstraction:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Value-centric abstraction (Type 1):</strong> Compares data by continuous metric distance in $\mathbb{R}^d$, enabling smooth local interpolation — the core strength of gradient-trained neural networks.</li>
    <li><strong>Program-centric abstraction (Type 2):</strong> Compares structures by exact discrete/algebraic isomorphism of generating programs (types, equivalences, compositional combinators) — enabling exact zero-shot out-of-distribution generalization across novel recombinations where continuous interpolation fails.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Hyperactivity resolves the 70-year schism between symbolic AI (McCarthy, 1959; Minsky, 1961; Newell &amp; Simon, 1976) and connectionism (Rosenblatt, 1958; Rumelhart et al., 1986): computational univalence (<code>ua</code>) makes program-level structural equivalence first-class identity, interaction nets evaluate programs with Lévy-optimal sharing ($\delta$) and local erasure ($\varepsilon$), and continuous homotopy intervals $I$ live natively alongside inductive types inside one verified kernel.</p>

  <h3 id="sec-7-3">7.3 Self-Reference, Lawvere's Fixed-Point Theorem, and Guarded Metacircularity</h3>
  <p>Any complete theory of intelligence must confront self-reference: an agent inspecting its own code, verifying its own verifier, or reasoning about the truth predicate of its own language. In <a href="/lawvere-fixed-points"><em>Lawvere's Fixed-Point Theorem</em></a> (<code>docs/lawvere-fixed-points.md</code>), Avik presents F. William Lawvere's (1969) unification of Cantor's theorem, Russell's paradox, Gödel's first incompleteness theorem (1931), Tarski's undefinability of truth (1936), and Turing's halting theorem (1936) in a single five-line constructive proof valid in any Cartesian Closed Category:</p>

  <div class="thm-box">
    <div class="thm-head"><span>Theorem 7.1 · Lawvere's Fixed-Point Theorem</span><span>Lawvere (1969); docs/lawvere-fixed-points.md</span></div>
    <p>In any Cartesian Closed Category with objects $A, B$, exponential $B^A$, and evaluation, if $\varphi : A \to B^A$ is <em>point-surjective</em> (every $g : 1 \to B^A$ equals $\varphi(a)$ for some $a : 1 \to A$), then every endomorphism $f : B \to B$ has a fixed point $b = f(b)$.</p>
    <p><em>Proof.</em> Define the diagonal $g : A \to B$ by $g(a) = f(\varphi(a)(a))$. By point-surjectivity, $g = \varphi(a_0)$ for some $a_0 : 1 \to A$. Evaluating at $a_0$ yields $\varphi(a_0)(a_0) = g(a_0) = f(\varphi(a_0)(a_0))$, so $b = \varphi(a_0)(a_0)$ is a fixed point of $f$. $\square$</p>
  </div>

  <p>By contrapositive, whenever $B$ admits a fixed-point-free endomorphism (such as Boolean negation $\neg : \mathbf{2} \to \mathbf{2}$), no point-surjective map $A \to B^A$ exists: $B = \mathbf{2}$ yields Cantor's $|A| \lt |\mathcal{P}(A)|$; $A =$ formulas and $f = \neg$ yields Gödel's incompleteness and Tarski's undefinability; $A =$ programs and $B =$ halting booleans yields Turing's halting undecidability. Because the proof uses neither the law of excluded middle nor choice, it holds constructively inside cubical type theory — showing both why unindexed two-valued global truth collapses under self-reference unless predication is indexed by standpoint (<em>catuṣkoṭi</em> in Nāgārjuna's <em>Mūlamadhyamakakārikā</em> and Jain <em>saptabhaṅgī</em> / <em>syādvāda</em>; Umāsvāti; Haldane, 1957; Matilal, 1981; Ganeri, 2002; Priest, 2008) and how productive self-reference works constructively via clocked guarded recursion ($\triangleright^\kappa$). In <code>lib/singularity.hypercubical</code>, this closes the self-hosting loop: a cold self-hosted pool verifier crawls <code>store/&lt;key&gt;/forms</code> directly from disk, masks each target definition's own global slot so circular self-justification is structurally impossible, checks <code>body : type</code>, implements productive guarded Lawvere + metacircular theory transport, and emits itself both as ISO C11 (<code>sg-c</code>) and as a zero-gcc static ELF64 binary (<code>sg-elf</code>).</p>

  <h3 id="sec-7-4">7.4 Meaning Derived from the Record and Language as Trained Synesthesia</h3>
  <p>What fixes the meaning of a word or symbol? Across Pāṇini's <em>Aṣṭādhyāyī</em>, Bhartṛhari's <em>Vākyapadīya</em>, Dignāga and Dharmakīrti's theory of <em>apoha</em> (determination by exclusion), Wittgenstein's <em>Philosophical Investigations</em> §43 ("the meaning of a word is its use in the language"), Harris (1954), and Firth (1957), linguistics established one law that neural word embeddings (Mikolov et al., 2013) approximated numerically: <strong>a word means its usage history in the record</strong>. In Hyperactivity, <code>lib/meaning.hypercubical</code> computes meaning directly from the content-addressed record without opaque weight distortion — making transparent why post-training that silently alters how models respond to politically or clinically contested words is literally minting institutional meaning by machine.</p>
  <p>In <a href="/language-is-trained-synesthesia"><em>Language Is Trained Synesthesia</em></a> (<code>docs/language-is-trained-synesthesia.md</code>; <code>neuro/synesthesia.hypercubical</code>), Avik proves an accompanying cognitive theorem: every literate human satisfies the clinical/neuroscience definition of synesthesia (automatic, involuntary, consistent cross-modal binding between visual graphemes and auditory phonology/semantics, measured directly in voice-selective auditory cortex during silent reading and via Stroop interference). Literacy is therefore a constructive planetary-scale proof of <strong>synthetic synesthesia</strong> — deliberate cognitive engineering of cross-modal homomorphisms over human perception, implemented directly across the site via <code>/synesthesia</code>.</p>
